WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
One small step of current, one voltage trace.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.340
What can it tell you about the cell?

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.230
For a compact, passive cell, it gives three numbers.

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.960
First, input resistance: the plateau divided by the current.

5
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.410
Second, the time constant: the time to reach 63% % of the plateau.

6
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:28.003
Third, capacitance: the time constant divided by the input resistance.

7
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:30.673
The word compact matters.

8
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:35.653
In a cell with big dendrites, that last step underestimates the capacitance.

9
00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:39.057
Give several small steps below threshold.

10
00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:41.737
Plot each plateau against its current.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.317
The slope is the input resistance.

12
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.600
A straight line fits a passive membrane.

13
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.640
But it does not prove one.

14
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.140
Now check the trace's shape.

15
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.560
A pure resistor and capacitor gives a smooth rise to a plateau.

16
00:00:57.683 --> 00:01:03.143
If the voltage overshoots and sags back during a hyperpolarizing step, a conductance

17
00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:04.363
is turning on with voltage.

18
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:07.353
Often it is the HCN current.

19
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:12.633
And if the plot of plateau against current bends, input resistance depends on

20
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:13.633
voltage.

21
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.107
Either way, a single resistor and capacitor fit will mislead you.

22
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.270
To predict a drug, ask two questions.

23
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.590
Which ion's conductance changed, and which way?

24
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.450
That moves rest.

25
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:29.703
And did the total conductance go up or down?

26
00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:33.223
That moves input resistance and the time constant.

27
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:38.027
A drug opens more potassium leak channels, and does nothing else.

28
00:01:38.887 --> 00:01:42.207
What happens to rest, input resistance, and the time constant?

29
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.360
Potassium's rope gets thicker, so rest moves toward potassium's mark.

30
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.360
The cell hyperpolarizes.

31
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.090
Total conductance rises.

32
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.290
So input resistance falls, and the time constant shortens.

33
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:06.963
Closing a leak does the reverse.

34
00:02:07.943 --> 00:02:12.943
Input resistance and the time constant rise, and rest moves away from that leak's

35
00:02:12.943 --> 00:02:13.943
reversal potential.

36
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.853
One case surprises most students.

37
00:02:16.853 --> 00:02:20.013
Suppose the new channels reverse right at rest.

38
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:24.177
Chloride channels do this when chloride sits at equilibrium.

39
00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:27.617
Their rope is anchored exactly where the marker sits.

40
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.170
The rope is not stretched, so it adds no pull.

41
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:32.990
Rest does not move.

42
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:35.933
But the marker is now harder to move.

43
00:02:36.773 --> 00:02:39.353
Any other current shifts the voltage less, and faster.

44
00:02:40.417 --> 00:02:45.957
That is shunting. It lowers input resistance without moving the resting potential.

45
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:49.817
So here is the whole rule, for our passive model.

46
00:02:50.677 --> 00:02:55.357
With no injected current, and the pump's own current set aside, rest is where the

47
00:02:55.357 --> 00:02:56.357
leaks balance.

48
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.940
And the leak, with capacitance and the core, decides how big, how fast, and how far

49
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.520
a nudge goes.
